---
import BlockLayout from '../../../layouts/BlockLayout.astro';
import { AFTER_THE_NOISE } from '../../../lib/pointcast-after-the-noise';
const description = 'After Florida beat Ole Miss 52–28, PointCast revisits its Week 4 conviction, moves Florida to No. 3 and Ole Miss to No. 12, and leaves the prior claim visible.';
const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'Article',
  headline: AFTER_THE_NOISE.title, alternativeHeadline: AFTER_THE_NOISE.subtitle,
  description, datePublished: AFTER_THE_NOISE.publishedAt,
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: AFTER_THE_NOISE.byline },
  url: AFTER_THE_NOISE.canonical, image: AFTER_THE_NOISE.image,
  isPartOf: { '@type': 'PublicationIssue', name: 'PointCast College Football — Issue 003: The Road Answer', url: 'https://pointcast.xyz/25/magazine' },
};
---
<BlockLayout title="After the Noise — PointCast College Football" description={description} image="/images/pointcast-25/after-the-noise.png" themeColor="#d3ff3e" jsonLd={jsonLd} immersive={true} showNetworkStrip={false} alternates={[{type:'application/json', href:'/25/magazine/after-the-noise.json', title:'After the Noise JSON'}]}>
  <main class="story">
    <nav class="story__nav" aria-label="Feature navigation"><a href="/">POINTCAST</a><a href="/25/magazine">Magazine</a><a href="/25">Current 25</a><a href="/25/receipts">Receipts</a></nav>
    <header class="story__header">
      <p class="kicker">ISSUE 003 / THE RECEIPT DESK / SEPTEMBER 29, 2026</p>
      <h1>AFTER<br /><em>THE NOISE.</em></h1>
      <p class="dek">{AFTER_THE_NOISE.subtitle}</p>
      <p class="byline">By {AFTER_THE_NOISE.byline} · Results through September 26</p>
    </header>
    <figure class="story__image"><img src="/images/pointcast-25/after-the-noise.png" width="1774" height="887" alt="Illustrated empty football stadium beneath dramatic night lights"/><figcaption>{AFTER_THE_NOISE.imageCredit}</figcaption></figure>
    <section class="story__body" aria-label="Article">
      <p class="lead">A week ago, Ole Miss gave our board its biggest correction. A week later, Florida gave us a correction to that correction. That is what a ranking with receipts is for.</p>
      <p>On September 19, Ole Miss beat LSU 32–24 in Oxford. PointCast moved the Rebels from No. 17 on the July opening board to No. 3 on Board 001. The leap was an editorial judgment, but it had a concrete trigger: a win over the kind of opponent that makes a September claim harder to dismiss. We wrote that the succession argument finally had a scoreboard.</p>
      <p>Then came Gainesville. Florida beat Ole Miss 52–28 on September 26. Florida’s postgame report records 302 team rushing yards and two second-half turnovers forced. Those details matter more than the margin alone: the Gators did not merely outlast a highly ranked visitor. They dictated long stretches of the game and took possession back when Ole Miss needed a response.</p>
      <div class="pull"><span>BOARD 001 → BOARD 002</span><strong>FLORIDA<br />18 → 3</strong><strong>OLE MISS<br />3 → 12</strong></div>
      <p>The first temptation is to say Ole Miss was never worthy of its rise. That would erase the LSU game. The second is to wave away the Florida result as a single bad Saturday. That would protect our prior sentence from the very proof it demanded. Both games count. Together, they say the Rebels had enough to beat LSU at home and have not yet shown the same command when a powerful opponent takes control on the road.</p>
      <p>Florida was No. 18 on our previous board. That was too low. The Gators are 4–0 after this result, and the manner of the win answers our earlier question about whether their physical game could travel from possibility to evidence against a strong opponent. Board 002 puts Florida at No. 3. The September 27 AP poll placed Florida No. 8 and Ole Miss No. 9; PointCast’s higher Florida placement is our own football judgment, not a reported consensus.</p>
      <p>Neither new number is a trophy. Florida’s next proof is sustaining that control when the opponent adjusts. Ole Miss needs a road answer against a team that can keep the same pressure on its offense. We will keep the older claims, the new doubts, and the dated boards accessible. The point of the board is not to have been right in July or last week. It is to show what changed our minds.</p>
    </section>
    <aside class="story__sources" aria-labelledby="sources-title"><h2 id="sources-title">On the record</h2><p>The game results and poll positions come from the linked sources. Rankings and interpretations are PointCast editorial judgments.</p><ul>{AFTER_THE_NOISE.sources.map(source => <li><a href={source.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{source.label} ↗</a></li>)}</ul></aside>
    <footer class="story__footer"><a href="/25/boards/001">Read Board 001</a><a href="/25/boards/002">Read Board 002</a><a href="/25/receipts">Open the receipt book</a><a href="/25/magazine">Back to magazine</a></footer>
  </main>
</BlockLayout>
